\title{Capped Valuation Dials, Corruption Budgets,\\ and the Erosion of a Rank Diagnostic}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a rank-correlation diagnostic --- the \emph{zero-fit dial} --- measuring the
Spearman correlation $\rho$ between the $2$-adic valuation $T(x)=v_2(x)$ of uniformly drawn
$b$-bit integers and a downstream numerical response. Across five experiments the dial reads
$0.780$, $0.705$, $0.648$, $0.608$ at bit-widths $b=44,52,64,76$, and $0.563/0.556$ on two
seeds at $b=92$, essentially at the validation floor $0.55$. We determine what can, and what
cannot, produce this monotone erosion, using exact combinatorics rather than statistics.

We compute the exact Spearman tie ceiling of the $K$-capped valuation $\min(v_2(x),K)$: for
$b=r+K\ge1$, $\rho^2_{\max}=\frac{6}{7}\frac{8^b-8^r}{8^b-2^b}=\frac67\frac{1-8^{-K}}{1-4^{-b}}$.
This \emph{capped resolution law} interpolates between $0$ and the dyadic ceiling
$\frac67(1+\frac1{2^b(2^b+1)})$, is strictly increasing in cap depth, and never drops below
$3/4$ for any $K\ge1$ and any $b$; since the readings satisfy $\rho^2\le0.317$, coarse
resolution is excluded. The exact tie-geometry budget between $b=52$ and $b=92$ is below
$10^{-15}$, against a measured drop above $0.14$. A Lipschitz estimate for Spearman
correlation under partial re-ranking converts readings into corruption budgets: a reading
$\rho$ forces displacement of at least a fraction $(1-\rho)/6$ of the sample, more than
$7.28\%$ at $b=92$; conversely a mechanism touching at most $3/40$ of the sample cannot push
the reading below $0.55$, so the validation floor \emph{is} the $7.5\%$ corruption budget.
A hyperbolic law $\rho(b)=\frac5{14}+\frac{93}{5b}$ fits all five readings to within $1/100$,
decreases to the sub-floor asymptote $5/14$, and crosses the floor exactly between $b=96$ and
$b=97$; along it the forced displacement equals $\frac3{28}-\frac{31}{10b}$ and saturates
strictly below $3/28$. Finally the capped theory generalises to every base $p\ge2$ with
$\rho^2_{\max}=L(p)\frac{p^{3b}-p^{3r}}{p^{3b}-p^b}$, $L(p)=\frac{3p}{p^2+p+1}$, and universal
capped floor $L(p)(1-p^{-3})$; defining the effective base of a reading by
$L(p+1)<\rho^2\le L(p)$, erosion is base drift --- base below $7$ at $b=76$, exactly $8$ at
$b=92$, and bounded forever strictly between $22$ and $23$ under the fitted law.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The diagnostic}

Fix a bit-width $b$ and draw integers uniformly from $\{0,1,\dots,2^b-1\}$. For each draw $x$
a computational pipeline produces a real-valued outcome, the \emph{rate}. The diagnostic under
study, the \emph{zero-fit dial}, is the Spearman rank correlation
\[
  \rho=\operatorname{Spearman}(T,\text{rate}),\qquad T(x)=v_2(x),
\]
where $v_2(x)$ is the $2$-adic valuation, the number of trailing zeros of $x$ in binary. The
dial's \emph{validation band} is $[0.55,0.85]$; readings below $0.55$ are declared
uninformative. The rationale is that a pipeline manipulating machine integers inherits the
$2$-adic structure of its inputs, so a strong monotone relationship between trailing zeros and
downstream behaviour indicates that the pipeline tracks arithmetic structure.

